<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  M18, PEReference round. A parameter entity reference is a USE, and the 24 corpus
  sites were producing no edge at all.

  `<!DOCTYPE root [ <!ENTITY % defroot '…'> %defroot; ]>` is how a DTD pulls in
  another one: `%defroot;` expands and the expansion's markup becomes part of this
  DTD. That is the same fact `entity_external_id` already records for
  `<!ENTITY % pe SYSTEM "common.dtd">`, reached by reference instead of by
  ExternalID — and it was recorded only for the ExternalID form. The owner is the
  DOCTYPE'S OWN NAME: a parameter reference in the internal subset has no element
  above it, which is the X7 shape — a claim the document makes, dropped because
  there was nothing obvious to attach it to.

  The replacement-text case is written the way libxml2's `result/valid/index.xml`
  writes it — a bare `%name;` on its own line inside a MULTI-LINE replacement text
  — because that is the shape the grammar accepts and the shape the corpus
  contains. A first draft wrote `%inner;<!ELEMENT outer (#PCDATA)>` with the
  reference butted straight against markup, which the grammar parses under an
  ERROR, and a fixture asserting an owner for a node the parser could not read
  would be pinning a fiction.

  THREE SHAPES, and the third is here because its discriminator from the second is
  a single character:

    1. a parameter reference in the internal subset  -> published, owned by the
       DOCTYPE
    2. `%name;` inside an entity's replacement text  -> published, owned by the
       DECLARING ENTITY, because a parameter entity invoking another is a
       dependency that entity has
    3. `% name 'replacement'` — the parameter-entity VALUE form, which names
       nothing and must produce NO edge. libxml2's corpus contains both `%xx;` and
       `% zz '&#60;`, and a rule that accepted either would invent names out of
       literal percent signs
-->
<!DOCTYPE root [
  <!ELEMENT root (#PCDATA)>
  <!ENTITY % inner "
		inner-content
		#IMPLIED
		">
  <!ENTITY % outer "
		%inner;
		outer-content
		#IMPLIED
		">
  <!ENTITY % literal "% zz '&#60;'">
  %outer;
  %neverDeclared;
]>
<root>text</root>